Below is a step-by-step recipe that works with the current **MapLibre GL JS v4+** (the same code also runs on v3-v6 because the `pmtiles` library ships a compatibility shim).

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles helper library

```bash
npm i pmtiles          # or yarn add pmtiles
```

`pmtiles` provides a **protocol handler** (`pmtiles://`) that makes a `.pmtiles` archive look like a normal TileJSON source to MapLibre.

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** (before any map is created)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

// Create the protocol object
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it under the name “pmtiles”
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);
```

> **Why only once?**  
> `addProtocol` registers a global handler. Registering it inside a component’s render loop will create duplicate handlers and can leak memory.

> **v4+ handler shape** – `pmtilesProtocol.tile` already knows the new async-/AbortController-based contract (`{data, cacheControl?, expires?}`), so you don’t have to write a wrapper yourself.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the archive

### Vector tiles (the most common case)

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // <-- the only source you need for the whole archive
      myPmtiles: {
        type: 'vector',
        // The “pmtiles://” prefix tells the protocol to use range requests
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
        // No `tiles:` array – the protocol reads the min/max zoom from the file header
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw water from the layer named "water" inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'water',
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },
      // Example: draw roads from the layer named "road"
      {
        id: 'roads',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': 1
        }
      }
      // …add as many layers as you like, each with its own `source-layer`
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

### Raster imagery

```js
// In the style JSON
{
  "rasterImagery": {
    "type": "raster",
    "url": "pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/imagery.pmtiles",
    "tileSize": 256   // optional, defaults to 256
  }
}
```

### Raster-DEM (terrain)

```js
{
  "terrain": {
    "type": "raster-dem",
    "url": "pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation.pmtiles",
    "encoding": "terrarium"   // or "mapbox"
  }
}
```

Then enable terrain on the map:

```js
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

---

## 4️⃣ Make sure the host can serve **HTTP Range** requests & CORS

| Host | What you need |
|------|----------------|
| **AWS S3 / Cloudflare R2** | Public read (or signed URL) + `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. Set `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000` for an immutable file. |
| **GitHub Pages / Netlify / Vercel** | Upload the `.pmtiles` file, verify the provider returns `Accept-Ranges`. Most static hosts do. |
| **CDN (CloudFront, Cloudflare, etc.)** | Put the file behind the CDN; range requests are cached automatically. |

**CORS header** – the response must contain `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) and `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range`. If you get a “blocked by CORS policy” error in the console, add those headers on the bucket or CDN edge.

---

## 5️⃣ Verify the archive and discover layer names

Before you point the style at the file, it’s handy to know the exact `source-layer` names:

```bash
# Install the official CLI (download binary from https://github.com/protomaps/go-pmtiles/releases)
pmtiles show world.pmtiles --metadata
```

You’ll see a `vector_layers` array, e.g.

```json
{
  "vector_layers": [
    { "id": "water", "fields": { "class": "String", "name": "String" } },
    { "id": "road",  "fields": { "class": "String", "name": "String" } },
    …
  ]
}
`` Use those `id`s in the style’s `"source-layer"` property.

---

## 6️⃣ (Optional) React / SPA setup

```tsx
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';

export default function Map() {
  useEffect(() => {
    // Register once when the app boots
    const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
    maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

    const map = new maplibregl.Map({
      container: 'map',
      style: {/* same style object as above */}
    });

    return () => {
      // Clean up when the last map is removed (prevents leaks)
      maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
      map.remove();
    };
  }, []);

  return <div id="map" style={{ width: '100%', height: '100%' }} />;
}
```

---

## 7️⃣ Common pitfalls & how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Blank vector tiles** (no features) | Used `tiles: ['pmtiles://…/{z}/{x}/{y}']` instead of `url:` | Switch to `url:` – the protocol reads the archive header and supplies the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`. |
| **404 / 500 on tile fetch** | Host does not support `Range` or CORS | Verify `Accept-Ranges: bytes` and `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` headers. |
| **MapLibre logs “Protocol handler returned undefined”** | Using an old `pmtiles` (< 3.0) that only supports callbacks | Upgrade to the latest `pmtiles` (≥ 3.0) or wrap the old handler in a promise that returns `{data}`. |
| **Over-zooming past the archive’s max zoom shows empty tiles** | You manually set `maxzoom` in the style or used `tiles:` template | Remove the manual `maxzoom` and let the protocol supply it from the file header. |
| **Large file (> 100 MB) on GitHub Pages fails** | GitHub Pages limits static assets to 100 MB | Host the file on S3/R2 or split the archive with `pmtiles extract`. |

---

## 8️⃣ Full minimal example (HTML + vanilla JS)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>PMTiles + MapLibre</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import * as maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
  import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/dist/index.min.js';

  // 1️⃣ Register the protocol
  const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
  maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

  // 2️⃣ Create the map
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        world: {
          type: 'vector',
          url: 'pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/world.pmtiles'
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'world',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: {
            'line-color': '#777',
            'line-width': 1
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page in a browser, and you should see the vector data rendered directly from the single `.pmtiles` file—no tile server required.

---

### TL;DR Checklist

1. `npm i pmtiles`
2. `maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', new pmtiles.Protocol().tile);`
3. In the style, use a **single source** with `type: 'vector' | 'raster' | 'raster-dem'` and `url: 'pmtiles://https://…/myfile.pmtiles'`.
4. Reference layers with `"source-layer": "<layer-id-from-metadata>"`.
5. Host the file on any static server that supports **Range** and **CORS**.
6. (Optional) Verify the archive with `pmtiles show <file> --metadata`.

That’s all you need to load a `.pmtiles` file as a source in MapLibre GL JS! 🚀

**Yes – you can serve a complete tile set from GitHub Pages with MapLibre GL JS and never touch a backend.**  
The trick is to put the tiles in a **PMTiles** archive (a single `.pmtiles` file) and let the browser fetch the needed byte-ranges from that static file. Below is a complete, step-by-step guide, plus the gotchas you’ll run into on GitHub Pages.

---

## 1. Why PMTiles + GitHub Pages works

| Feature | What you need | How PMTiles satisfies it |
|---------|---------------|--------------------------|
| **Static-only host** | No server-side code, just files served over HTTP | A PMTiles file is just a binary blob. Any HTTP server that supports **Range** requests can serve individual tiles from it. |
| **CORS** | Browsers will request the file from a different origin (your site → raw.githubusercontent.com) | GitHub Pages automatically adds `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (and `Accept-Ranges: bytes`), so the browser can read the file. |
| **Cacheability** | You want the file to be cached forever (tiles don’t change often) | Set a long `Cache-Control` header in the repo’s `.gitattributes` (or via a GitHub Actions step) – the CDN edge will cache the whole file and each range request. |
| **Size limit** | GitHub Pages only serves files ≤ 100 MiB (raw file size) | Split a very large dataset into several regional PMTiles files, or keep the archive ≤ 100 MiB. |
| **No tile-server logic** | No need to translate `z/x/y` → file path | The **pmtiles** JavaScript library implements the `pmtiles://` protocol and does the range-request math for you. |

---

## 2. Create a PMTiles archive

You can start from any source (GeoJSON, Shapefile, OSM, raster imagery). The easiest path is:

1. **Generate MBTiles** (most tile generators output MBTiles).  
   Example with **tippecanoe** (vector) or **gdal2tiles** (raster):
   ```bash
   # Vector example – GeoJSON → MBTiles
   tippecanoe -zg -o mydata.mbtiles mydata.geojson

   # Raster example – GeoTIFF → MBTiles (GDAL ≥3.8)
   gdal_translate -of MBTILES -co TILE_FORMAT=PNG -co ZLEVEL=9 myimage.tif myimage.mbtiles
   ```

2. **Convert to PMTiles** (single command, no extra dependencies):
   ```bash
   # Download the pmtiles CLI (one-off)
   curl -L https://github.com/protomaps/go-pmtiles/releases/download/v2.5.0/pmtiles-linux-amd64 -o pmtiles
   chmod +x pmtiles

   # Convert
   ./pmtiles convert mydata.mbtiles mydata.pmtiles
   ```

3. **Verify & inspect** (optional but recommended):
   ```bash
   ./pmtiles show mydata.pmtiles --metadata   # shows bounds, zoom range, layer names
   ./pmtiles verify mydata.pmtiles            # exits 0 if the archive is healthy
   ```

> **Tip:** If the resulting file is > 100 MiB, run `pmtiles extract` to clip to a bounding box or a smaller zoom range, or split the region into multiple files (e.g. `europe.pmtiles`, `asia.pmtiles`).

---

## 3. Put the file on GitHub Pages

1. **Create a repo** (public or private – GitHub Pages works for both).  
   If you already have a site repo, just add the file there.

2. **Add the PMTiles file** (binary).  
   ```bash
   git lfs install               # optional – if you want LFS for > 50 MiB files
   git add mydata.pmtiles
   git commit -m "Add PMTiles archive"
   git push origin main
   ```

   *GitHub Pages will serve the raw file from `https://<username>.github.io/<repo>/mydata.pmtiles`.*  
   If you use **Git LFS**, the raw URL still works; GitHub’s LFS endpoint supports range requests.

3. **Enable GitHub Pages** (Settings → Pages → Source → `main` branch / `/ (root)`).  
   After a minute you’ll have a base URL, e.g. `https://username.github.io/your-repo/`.

4. **(Optional) Set long-term caching** – GitHub Pages does not let you edit response headers directly, but you can force a long cache by **adding a `.gitattributes` rule** that tells the CDN to treat the file as immutable:

   ```gitattributes
   mydata.pmtiles export-ignore
   mydata.pmtiles filter=lfs diff=lfs merge=lfs -text
   ```

   The more reliable way is to use a **GitHub Action** that runs after each push and adds a `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable` header via the `github-pages-deploy-action`. For most static sites the default caching (a few hours) is fine; the heavy lifting is done by the CDN edge anyway.

---

## 4. Wire the archive into MapLibre GL JS

### 4.1 Install the pmtiles library

```bash
npm install pmtiles maplibre-gl
# or, if you use Yarn / pnpm
```

### 4.2 Register the protocol **once** (e.g. in your app’s entry point)

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create the protocol handler
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Register it globally – do this before any MapLibre map is constructed
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);
```

> **Why `addProtocol` matters** – Since MapLibre GL JS v4 the handler must return an object `{data, cacheControl?, expires?}`. `protocol.tile` already does that, so you don’t need a custom wrapper.

### 4.3 Define a style that points at the archive

You can embed the style directly in JavaScript (as in the example below) or host a separate `style.json`. The crucial part is **using `url:` (not `tiles:`)** so the protocol can read the archive’s internal TileJSON and get the correct `minzoom`/`maxzoom`.

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // <div id="map"></div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // Vector example
      mytiles: {
        type: 'vector',
        // The `pmtiles://` prefix tells the protocol to treat the URL as a PMTiles file
        url: 'pmtiles://https://username.github.io/your-repo/mydata.pmtiles'
      },

      // Raster example (uncomment if you have a raster PMTiles)
      // imagery: {
      //   type: 'raster',
      //   url: 'pmtiles://https://username.github.io/your-repo/imagery.pmtiles',
      //   tileSize: 256
      // },

      // Raster-DEM example (Terrarium encoding)
      // terrain: {
      //   type: 'raster-dem',
      //   url: 'pmtiles://https://username.github.io/your-repo/elevation.pmtiles',
      //   encoding: 'terrarium'
      // }
    },
    layers: [
      // Background (optional)
      {
        id: 'bg',
        type: 'background',
        paint: { 'background-color': '#f0f0f0' }
      },

      // Example vector layer – replace `water` with a layer name from `pmtiles show --metadata`
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'mytiles',
        'source-layer': 'water',
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },

      // Example road layer
      {
        id: 'roads',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'mytiles',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 5, 0.5, 15, 4]
        }
      }

      // Add more layers (labels, POIs, etc.) as needed.
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

**Key points in the style:**

* `type: 'vector'` (or `'raster'` / `'raster-dem'`) matches the content of the PMTiles file.
* `url: 'pmtiles://https://…/mydata.pmtiles'` – the `pmtiles://` scheme triggers the protocol handler.
* **Never use** `tiles: ['https://…/{z}/{x}/{y}.pmtiles']`. That bypasses the archive header and forces MapLibre to assume a default `maxzoom: 22`, which leads to blank tiles when the archive stops at a lower zoom.
* Each layer that draws from the same archive uses the same `source` name (`mytiles`) but a different `"source-layer"` (the internal layer name).

### 4.4 (Optional) Add terrain, glyphs, sprites

If you want 3-D terrain:

```js
map.setTerrain({
  source: 'terrain', // the source defined above
  exaggeration: 1.5
});
```

For labels you’ll also need a **glyphs** URL and a **sprite** sheet (both can be hosted on GitHub Pages as well). Example:

```js
style: {
  // …
  glyphs: 'https://username.github.io/your-repo/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf',
  sprite: 'https://username.github.io/your-repo/sprite',
  // …
}
```

---

## 5. Verify it works locally before pushing

1. **Run a local static server** (e.g. `npx serve .` or `python -m http.server`).  
   This mimics the GitHub Pages environment (range requests, CORS).  
2. Open the page, open the browser dev-tools → **Network** tab, filter for `mydata.pmtiles`.  
   You should see a **single GET** for the whole file (or a small range request for the header) and then **range requests** for each tile (`Range: bytes=…`).  
3. If you see `206 Partial Content` responses and the map draws, you’re good to go.

---

## 6. Common pitfalls on GitHub Pages and how to fix them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| **Map stays gray, console shows `Failed to fetch`** | CORS missing or blocked. | GitHub Pages already sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`. If you’re using a **private repo** with LFS, make sure the LFS URL is public or use a personal access token in the URL (not recommended for client-side). |
| **`Range` header not honored, whole file downloaded each tile** | The file is being served with `Content-Encoding: gzip` (GitHub compresses static files). Range requests on a gzipped response are broken. | **Do not gzip** the `.pmtiles` file. GitHub Pages automatically disables gzip for files it detects as binary (including `.pmtiles`). If you see `Content-Encoding: gzip` in the network tab, rename the file to `.pmtiles.bin` and reference that name, or add a `.gitattributes` entry: `*.pmtiles -text -filter` to force raw delivery. |
| **404 on the PMTiles URL** | Wrong URL (GitHub Pages uses the repo name as a path). | Verify the exact URL by opening it directly in a browser: `https://username.github.io/your-repo/mydata.pmtiles`. |
| **File > 100 MiB, GitHub rejects push** | GitHub Pages size limit. | Split the dataset, or host the large file on a different static bucket (e.g.

## What changed in MapLibre GL JS v4?

`maplibregl.addProtocol()` used to accept a **callback-style** handler:

```js
// v3 (and earlier)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

Starting with **v4** the API became **promise-based** and the handler now receives an **`AbortController`** instead of a callback.  
The handler **must resolve to an object that contains a `data` property** (the raw tile bytes).  
If you return a bare `ArrayBuffer`, `Blob`, `Uint8Array`, or `Response`, MapLibre will treat the tile as “no data” and you’ll see blank tiles (or a silent failure).

```js
// v4+ signature
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  // …fetch tile…
  // return { data: <ArrayBuffer|Uint8Array|Blob|Response>, cacheControl?, expires? }
});
```

Because of that change, any code that still uses the old callback signature will stop working after the upgrade.

---

## Quick fix for a custom tile protocol

### 1️⃣ Replace the callback with an async function

```js
// BEFORE (v3)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  fetch(params.url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
    .catch(err => callback(err));
});
```

```js
// AFTER (v4+)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', async (params, abortController) => {
  // Abort the request if the map cancels it (e.g. user pans away)
  const response = await fetch(params.url, { signal: abortController.signal });

  if (!response.ok) {
    // Throwing aborts the tile request and MapLibre will treat it as an error
    throw new Error(`Tile fetch failed: ${response.status} ${response.statusText}`);
  }

  // You can return any of the following as `data`:
  //   - ArrayBuffer
  //   - Uint8Array
  //   - Blob
  //   - Response (the whole fetch response)
  const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();

  // The contract requires the wrapper object:
  return { data: buffer };
});
```

**Key points**

| What you must do | Why |
|------------------|-----|
| **Return `{ data: … }`** | MapLibre extracts the tile bytes from the `data` field. |
| **Accept `abortController`** | Allows the request to be cancelled when the map no longer needs the tile (e.g., fast pan/zoom). |
| **Throw on error** | Throwing (or returning a rejected promise) signals a tile-load error; MapLibre will show the “error tile” or retry. |
| **Do not call a callback** | The callback argument is no longer passed; using it will be a no-op and the tile will be empty. |

### 2️⃣ Optional: Preserve the old callback-style code with a shim

If you have many existing handlers and want a quick “drop-in” shim, you can wrap the old callback API in a tiny adapter:

```js
function v3toV4Adapter(callbackHandler) {
  return async (params, abortController) => {
    // Return a promise that resolves when the callback is called
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      // The old handler expects (params, callback)
      callbackHandler(params, (err, data) => {
        if (err) return reject(err);
        resolve({ data });          // wrap in {data}
      });

      // If the map aborts the request, abort the underlying fetch (if any)
      abortController.signal.addEventListener('abort', () => {
        // If your callback uses fetch, it will already respect the signal.
        // Otherwise you can reject here:
        reject(new Error('Tile request aborted'));
      });
    });
  };
}

// Example usage
maplibregl.addProtocol(
  'my',
  v3toV4Adapter((params, callback) => {
    fetch(params.url)
      .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
      .then(buf => callback(null, buf))
      .catch(err => callback(err));
  })
);
```

> **Note:** The shim works for simple fetch-based handlers, but if your old code does its own XHR/Abort handling you’ll need to adapt it accordingly.

### 3️⃣ If you’re already using the **pmtiles** library, you don’t need to change anything

The `pmtiles` package ships a **v3-compat shim** inside `new pmtiles.Protocol().tile`.  
That shim detects whether the second argument is an `AbortController` (v4) or a callback (v3) and returns the correctly-shaped `{data,…}` object. So the classic one-liner still works:

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);   // works on v3, v4, v5, v6
```

If you’re **not** using `pmtiles` and you have a custom source, follow the async-handler pattern above.

---

## Full example – a custom vector-tile source hosted on S3 (or any static host)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre with a custom protocol (v4+)</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
  import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';

  // ---- 1️⃣ Register a custom protocol ----
  maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abortController) => {
    // Example: tiles are stored as <z>/<x>/<y>.pbf in an S3 bucket
    const url = `https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/${params.z}/${params.x}/${params.y}.pbf`;

    const response = await fetch(url, { signal: abortController.signal });
    if (!response.ok) {
      throw new Error(`Tile ${params.z}/${params.x}/${params.y} not found`);
    }
    const buffer = await response.arrayBuffer();
    return { data: buffer };
  });

  // ---- 2️⃣ Create the map ----
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {
        // Use the custom protocol in the source URL
        mySource: {
          type: 'vector',
          // `url` is a TileJSON endpoint; we can also use `tiles` with a template.
          // Here we use the protocol directly:
          url: 'mytiles://https://example.com/does-not-matter' // the URL part is ignored; we build our own inside the handler
        }
      },
      layers: [
        {
          id: 'water',
          type: 'fill',
          source: 'mySource',
          'source-layer': 'water',
          paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
        },
        {
          id: 'roads',
          type: 'line',
          source: 'mySource',
          'source-layer': 'road',
          paint: { 'line-color': '#777', 'line-width': 1 }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2
  });

  // ---- 3️⃣ Clean-up (optional) ----
  // If you ever destroy the map and never create another one,
  // you can deregister the protocol to avoid memory leaks:
  // map.on('remove', () => maplibregl.removeProtocol('mytiles'));
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

### What the code does

| Step | Explanation |
|------|-------------|
| **Register protocol** | `addProtocol('mytiles', async …)` receives `params` (`{z,x,y,url}`) and an `AbortController`. It builds the real tile URL, fetches it with the abort signal, and returns `{data: ArrayBuffer}`. |
| **Use the protocol** | In the style, `url: 'mytiles://…'` tells MapLibre to invoke the handler. The part after `mytiles://` is ignored by the handler (you can embed a base URL if you like). |
| **Clean-up** | `removeProtocol` is optional but good practice if you ever unload the map without a page refresh. |

---

## Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |
|---------|--------------|-----|
| Tiles appear blank (no error in console) | Handler returns a raw `ArrayBuffer` instead of `{data: …}` | Wrap the buffer: `return { data: buffer };` |
| “Tile request aborted” errors in console | You ignored the `abortController` and the fetch kept running after the map cancelled it. | Pass `{ signal: abortController.signal }` to `fetch`. |
| “NetworkError when attempting to fetch resource.” (CORS) | Host does not send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` or `Accept-Ranges`. | Ensure the static host (S3, R2, GitHub Pages, etc.) returns `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) **and** `Accept-Ranges: bytes`. |
| “Failed to load source …: TileJSON missing `tiles` or `url`” | You used `tiles:` instead of `url:` for a PMTiles source, bypassing the header-derived zoom range. | For PMTiles always use `url:` (e.g., `url: 'pmtiles://https://example.com/tiles.pmtiles'`). |
| “Uncaught (in promise) TypeError: Cannot read property 'data' of undefined” | Your custom handler resolved to a plain buffer or `Response` without the `{data}` wrapper. | Return `{ data: … }`. |
| “Protocol handler not found” | You called `addProtocol` **after** the map was already constructed, or you miss-spelled the protocol name. | Register the protocol **before** any `new maplibregl.Map()` runs, and double-check the string (`'mytiles'`). |

---

## If you already use the **pmtiles** library – keep the one-liner

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();   // v3-compatible shim built-in
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);
```

*Why it works on v4+*  
`protocol.tile` detects the signature (`params, abortController`) and internally returns `{data, cacheControl?, expires?}`. No code changes are required.

---

## Recap – steps to migrate your custom protocol

1. **Change the handler signature** to `async (params, abortController) => { … }`.
2. **Fetch with the abort signal** (`fetch(url, { signal: abortController.signal })`).
3. **Return an object** `{ data: <ArrayBuffer|Uint8Array|Blob|Response> }`.
4. **Throw on errors** (or return a rejected promise) so MapLibre can surface the failure.
5. **Register the protocol before creating any map**.
6. (Optional) Use the `pmtiles` shim if you’re serving PMTiles – no changes needed.

That’s all you need to get your custom tile source working again after the MapLibre GL JS v4 upgrade. Happy mapping! 🚀

**Short answer:** No – PMTiles is a *static* archive. It’s perfect for basemap tiles that never change, but it isn’t a good fit for data that updates every 30 seconds from a live PostGIS database.

Below is a quick why-and-how guide, plus a concrete recipe you can copy-paste into a MapLibre app.

---

## 1️⃣ Why PMTiles won’t work for live bus positions

| Feature | PMTiles | Live bus feed (PostGIS → MapLibre) |
|---------|---------|-----------------------------------|
| **File-based** | One `.pmtiles` file on static storage. | Data lives in a database; each request must read the latest rows. |
| **Update model** | To change a tile you have to rebuild the archive and re-upload the whole file (or a new file). | You only need to add a few rows (or update a few geometries) every 30 s. |
| **Cache-ability** | The whole archive is cached aggressively (often `max-age=1 yr`). | You need a short-lived cache or no cache at all so the client sees fresh positions. |
| **Network pattern** | Client makes *range* requests for a single tile (byte offsets). | Client needs a *fresh* response for each poll (or push) – usually a JSON or vector-tile request that reflects the current DB state. |
| **Complexity** | Zero server logic – just static hosting. | Requires a tile-serving layer (or a GeoJSON endpoint) that can query PostGIS on demand. |

In short, you would have to rebuild and re-upload a whole PMTiles file every 30 seconds – which defeats the purpose of a “single-file, zero-ops” format.

---

## 2️⃣ Recommended architectures for a 30-second bus feed

### A. **Vector-tile server that reads PostGIS on-the-fly**
*Examples:*  

| Server | Language | How it works |
|--------|----------|--------------|
| **pg_tileserv** | Go | Exposes `/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` that runs a SQL query for each tile. |
| **t-rex** | Rust | Same idea – fast, supports CORS, can serve raster or vector tiles. |
| **Martin** (MapLibre’s own server) | Go | Can read from S3, MBTiles, or a PostGIS-backed TileJSON source. |
| **tileserver-gl** | Node | Can be pointed at a PostGIS connection via `postgis` driver. |

**How to wire it up**

```json
// style.json (or inline style)
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "bus": {
      "type": "vector",
      "tiles": [
        "https://my-tileserver.example.com/bus/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"
      ],
      "minzoom": 0,
      "maxzoom": 14
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "bus-points",
      "type": "circle",
      "source": "bus",
      "source-layer": "bus_positions",   // whatever you name the layer in the SQL
      "paint": {
        "circle-radius": 6,
        "circle-color": "#ff6600",
        "circle-stroke-width": 1,
        "circle-stroke-color": "#fff"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*Pros*  

* Tiles are cached by the browser/CDN for a few seconds (you can set `Cache-Control: max-age=30`).  
* Only the tiles that intersect the viewport are fetched, keeping bandwidth low.  
* Works exactly like any other vector-tile source – you can still use a static PMTiles basemap for the background.

*Cons*  

* You need to run a tiny server (few MB of RAM, < 1 CPU core).  
* You must expose the DB (or a read-only replica) to that server.

---

### B. **GeoJSON (or NDJSON) endpoint with polling**
If the bus dataset is modest (< 10 k points) you can skip tiles altogether and just fetch a GeoJSON feature collection every 30 s.

```js
// 1️⃣ Register a GeoJSON source (once)
map.addSource('bus-geojson', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: { type: 'FeatureCollection', features: [] } // empty placeholder
});

// 2️⃣ Add a layer that draws the points
map.addLayer({
  id: 'bus-points',
  type: 'circle',
  source: 'bus-geojson',
  paint: {
    'circle-radius': 5,
    'circle-color': '#0066ff',
    'circle-stroke-width': 1,
    'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
  }
});

// 3️⃣ Poll the endpoint every 30 s
async function refreshBuses() {
  try {
    const resp = await fetch('https://my-api.example.com/bus-positions.geojson', {
      // CORS headers must be allowed on the server
      credentials: 'omit'
    });
    const geojson = await resp.json();
    map.getSource('bus-geojson').setData(geojson);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Bus refresh failed', e);
  }
}
refreshBuses();                     // initial load
setInterval(refreshBuses, 30_000);  // repeat every 30 s
```

*Pros*  

* No tile server needed – just a tiny HTTP endpoint that runs a `SELECT … FROM bus_positions`.  
* Simpler to debug (you can open the URL in a browser and see raw GeoJSON).  

*Cons*  

* The whole dataset is transferred each poll, which can be wasteful if you have many points.  
* No built-in level-of-detail – you’ll see all points even when zoomed out (you can filter on the server side, but you lose the “only-visible-tiles” efficiency of vector tiles).

---

### C. **Hybrid: static PMTiles basemap + live bus source**
You *can* keep a PMTiles file for the **basemap** (roads, landuse, etc.) and use either **A** or **B** for the moving buses. This gives you the best of both worlds:

```json
{
  "sources": {
    "basemap": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "pmtiles://https://example.com/europe-basemap.pmtiles"
    },
    "bus": { /* either vector-tile server or geojson as above */ }
  },
  "layers": [
    /* basemap layers … */,
    { "id": "bus-points", "type": "circle", "source": "bus", "source-layer": "bus_positions", … }
  ]
}
```

---

## 3️⃣ Quick checklist before you start

| ✅ Item | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|
| **CORS** on the live endpoint (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain). | MapLibre runs in the browser; without CORS the fetch fails. |
| **Cache-Control** on the live tiles/GeoJSON (`max-age=30` or `no-cache`). | Prevents the browser from serving a stale 30-second snapshot. |
| **HTTPS** everywhere. | Modern browsers block mixed-content range requests. |
| **Source-layer name** matches what your server emits (`source-layer` in the style). | If you use a custom SQL view, give it an alias (`SELECT … AS bus_positions`). |
| **TileJSON `minzoom`/`maxzoom`** – either let the server emit a TileJSON or set them manually. | Guarantees MapLibre won’t request zoom levels that don’t exist. |
| **Rate-limit** on the DB side (e.g., a read-only replica). | A 30-second poll from many clients can add up; keep the query cheap (`SELECT id, ST_AsMVTGeom(geom, …) FROM bus_positions WHERE …`). |

---

## 4️⃣ TL;DR – What you should do

1. **Keep your static basemap in a PMTiles file** (hosted on S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, etc.) – this gives you a fast, zero-ops background.
2. **Serve the bus positions with a live tile server** (e.g., `pg_tileserv` or `t-rex`) **or** a simple GeoJSON endpoint that you poll every 30 seconds.
3. Add the live source to your MapLibre style as a separate source, and style it with a `circle` (or `symbol`) layer.
4. Make sure CORS and cache headers are correct, and you’re done.

---

### Minimal example (static basemap + live GeoJSON)

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Bus map – PMTiles basemap + live positions</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map {position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script type="module">
import maplibregl from 'https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js';
import * as pmtiles from 'https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@3.0.0/+esm';

// 1️⃣ Register PMTiles protocol (once)
const protocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', protocol.tile);

// 2️⃣ Create the map
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      basemap: {
        type: 'vector',
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/europe-basemap.pmtiles'
      },
      bus: {
        type: 'geojson',
        data: { type: 'FeatureCollection', features: [] }
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // …basemap layers (you can copy them from the PMTiles TileJSON)…
      {
        id: 'bus-points',
        type: 'circle',
        source: 'bus',
        paint: {
          'circle-radius': 5,
          'circle-color': '#ff6600',
          'circle-stroke-width': 1,
          'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  center: [-73.985, 40.748],
  zoom: 12
});

// 3️⃣ Poll the live endpoint
async function refreshBuses() {
  try {
    const r = await fetch('https://my-api.example.com/bus-positions.geojson');
    const geo = await r.json();
    map.getSource('bus').setData(geo);
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('Failed to fetch bus positions', e);
  }
}
refreshBuses();
setInterval(refreshBuses, 30_000);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace the URLs with your own S3 bucket and API endpoint.* The map will load the static PMTiles basemap instantly, then update the bus points every 30 seconds without ever touching the PMTiles file again.

---

### Bottom line

- **PMTiles = static, immutable tile archive.**  
- **Live bus positions = mutable, need a dynamic source.**  

Use PMTiles for the background only, and pair it with a live tile server or a polling GeoJSON endpoint for the moving data. That gives you the performance of a single-file basemap *and* the freshness you need for
